1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,380
This is a log.

2
00:00:01,865 --> 00:00:03,245
And so is this.

3
00:00:03,752 --> 00:00:08,152
Somehow, a piece of wood became something
your computer writes all day long.

4
00:00:08,291 --> 00:00:09,911
And the story of how it got there involves

5
00:00:10,141 --> 00:00:13,371
a ship, a rope, and a
sailor counting knots.

6
00:00:14,483 --> 00:00:16,523
Computers are full of words like this.

7
00:00:16,603 --> 00:00:20,803
Mouse. Desktop. Cloud. Bug.

8
00:00:20,883 --> 00:00:24,383
Words that meant something else
long before there were computers.

9
00:00:25,211 --> 00:00:26,711
Some of them are obvious.

10
00:00:27,251 --> 00:00:29,331
Some were chosen very carefully.

11
00:00:29,411 --> 00:00:34,351
And some have travelled so far that hardly
anyone remembers where they started.

12
00:00:36,631 --> 00:00:38,671
Let's start with the easy ones.

13
00:00:39,415 --> 00:00:42,975
The mouse. It's small, it
sits on your desk, and the

14
00:00:43,205 --> 00:00:45,595
early ones had a cord
coming out of the back.

15
00:00:45,911 --> 00:00:49,991
In the 1960s, the team that built it
thought the cord looked like a tail.

16
00:00:50,631 --> 00:00:54,791
Nobody remembers who said it
first. But the name stuck.

17
00:00:55,441 --> 00:00:58,101
It wasn't a plan. It
just looked like a mouse.

18
00:01:00,481 --> 00:01:02,581
The desktop is a different story.

19
00:01:03,161 --> 00:01:06,681
Your computer has files,
inside folders, on a desktop.

20
00:01:07,481 --> 00:01:09,741
And when you're done with
them, they go in the bin.

21
00:01:10,373 --> 00:01:12,293
None of that is an accident.

22
00:01:12,893 --> 00:01:15,773
In the 1970s, researchers
at Xerox designed

23
00:01:16,003 --> 00:01:18,513
screens that deliberately
looked like an office.

24
00:01:19,413 --> 00:01:21,213
Apple later added the bin.

25
00:01:21,422 --> 00:01:23,002
Instead of teaching people a whole new

26
00:01:23,232 --> 00:01:29,042
vocabulary, they gave them one they
already knew. Desk. Folder. Document. Bin.

27
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:33,080
There's no desk inside your computer.

28
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:35,860
And there's definitely no tiny bin.

29
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:38,420
But you know exactly what they mean.

30
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:40,420
That was the whole point.

31
00:01:41,103 --> 00:01:44,583
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

32
00:01:45,203 --> 00:01:46,823
It really helps the channel.

33
00:01:48,993 --> 00:01:51,713
And that idea, using something
you can see to explain

34
00:01:51,943 --> 00:01:55,373
something you can't, is
behind most of these words.

35
00:01:56,129 --> 00:01:57,329
Take the cloud.

36
00:01:57,931 --> 00:02:00,671
Long before anyone kept
photos in it, engineers

37
00:02:00,901 --> 00:02:03,571
drawing network diagrams had a problem.

38
00:02:04,091 --> 00:02:05,991
Somewhere in the middle was a huge,

39
00:02:06,221 --> 00:02:09,071
complicated network they
didn't need to show.

40
00:02:09,277 --> 00:02:13,717
So they drew a cloud. A fuzzy shape
that meant: there's a lot going

41
00:02:13,947 --> 00:02:16,237
on in here, and you
don't need to know what.

42
00:02:16,841 --> 00:02:18,961
That's still exactly what it means.

43
00:02:19,681 --> 00:02:21,501
Your files in the cloud aren't in the sky.

44
00:02:21,731 --> 00:02:25,141
They're on somebody
else's computers, probably

45
00:02:25,371 --> 00:02:28,221
in a data centre, possibly
on another continent.

46
00:02:29,080 --> 00:02:31,960
The cloud is simply the
part you don't need to see.

47
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:36,040
The web is a picture too.

48
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:41,880
When Tim Berners-Lee named the World Wide
Web in 1990, he was describing a shape.

49
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:46,320
Documents linked to other documents,
with no centre and no edge.

50
00:02:46,969 --> 00:02:50,429
Like a spider's web, every
thread leads to another thread.

51
00:02:51,049 --> 00:02:54,769
And, just like a fly, you can easily
get stuck in it for three hours.

52
00:02:57,219 --> 00:02:58,859
Then there's the packet.

53
00:02:59,362 --> 00:03:04,002
In the mid-1960s, a British
scientist, Donald Davies, needed

54
00:03:04,232 --> 00:03:07,362
a name for small pieces of data
travelling across a network.

55
00:03:08,098 --> 00:03:11,458
He chose packet, a small
parcel, partly because the

56
00:03:11,688 --> 00:03:14,358
word would translate easily
into other languages.

57
00:03:14,749 --> 00:03:15,949
And it fits.

58
00:03:16,409 --> 00:03:19,969
When you watch a video online,
you're not receiving it in one piece.

59
00:03:20,569 --> 00:03:22,589
You're getting hundreds
of thousands of tiny

60
00:03:22,819 --> 00:03:25,569
parcels, each with an
address on the front.

61
00:03:26,577 --> 00:03:31,157
Which brings us to another word
from the world of deliveries. Load.

62
00:03:31,965 --> 00:03:33,665
A truck carries a load.

63
00:03:33,805 --> 00:03:38,425
But not everything a truck hauls around
is cargo someone has paid to send.

64
00:03:39,225 --> 00:03:42,325
There's fuel, there's the
driver, there's the truck itself.

65
00:03:43,137 --> 00:03:47,457
The part that actually earns money
is the pay load. The payload.

66
00:03:47,970 --> 00:03:49,550
Rockets kept the word.

67
00:03:50,130 --> 00:03:52,830
A rocket is mostly fuel,
and the payload is the

68
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:55,810
small part on top that
the whole launch is for.

69
00:03:56,409 --> 00:03:58,589
A network packet is no different.

70
00:03:59,229 --> 00:04:02,809
Every packet has a label, with an
address and delivery instructions.

71
00:04:02,989 --> 00:04:04,589
And a payload.

72
00:04:04,749 --> 00:04:06,769
The bit you actually wanted.

73
00:04:09,309 --> 00:04:11,729
Now let's move from the road to the water.

74
00:04:12,499 --> 00:04:13,779
A stream flows.

75
00:04:14,419 --> 00:04:18,379
You don't have to wait for the whole river
to arrive before you can drink from it.

76
00:04:18,911 --> 00:04:23,631
That's streaming. The film starts playing
while the rest of it is still on the way.

77
00:04:23,979 --> 00:04:27,639
But water doesn't always arrive
evenly, and neither does data.

78
00:04:28,179 --> 00:04:29,659
So we need a buffer.

79
00:04:30,391 --> 00:04:32,791
A buffer is the thing on the
end of a railway carriage

80
00:04:33,021 --> 00:04:36,311
that takes the jolt when
it bumps into the next one.

81
00:04:36,866 --> 00:04:39,146
A data buffer does the same job.

82
00:04:39,806 --> 00:04:44,326
It absorbs the bumps, so the video doesn't
stop every time the connection stumbles.

83
00:04:44,728 --> 00:04:47,548
And when data has to pass
through several stages,

84
00:04:47,628 --> 00:04:50,588
one after another, programmers
call it a pipeline.

85
00:04:51,037 --> 00:04:52,977
That one was plumbing from the start.

86
00:04:53,857 --> 00:04:57,197
In 1964, Doug McIlroy
at Bell Labs wrote that

87
00:04:57,427 --> 00:05:00,237
programs should connect
like lengths of garden hose.

88
00:05:00,970 --> 00:05:04,010
Data flows, gets buffered,
and runs through pipes.

89
00:05:04,530 --> 00:05:08,110
Somewhere along the way, computing
quietly turned into plumbing.

90
00:05:10,710 --> 00:05:12,270
And then there's padding.

91
00:05:13,025 --> 00:05:17,725
Padding is what you put around something
to protect it. A padded jacket.

92
00:05:18,285 --> 00:05:20,465
A padded envelope. A padded chair.

93
00:05:21,189 --> 00:05:23,509
On a web page, padding
is the space between

94
00:05:23,739 --> 00:05:26,409
the edge of a box and the words inside it.

95
00:05:27,029 --> 00:05:31,049
Almost every button you've ever clicked
has a little cushion around the text.

96
00:05:31,419 --> 00:05:33,959
And outside the padding
is the margin, a word

97
00:05:34,189 --> 00:05:36,379
borrowed straight from the edge of a page.

98
00:05:38,809 --> 00:05:41,769
So far, all of these words
have something in common.

99
00:05:42,349 --> 00:05:44,409
You can see the picture behind them.

100
00:05:44,809 --> 00:05:47,069
Once someone points it out, it's obvious.

101
00:05:47,890 --> 00:05:52,490
But some words have travelled so far that
the original picture has completely faded.

102
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:57,180
Remember the log?

103
00:05:58,308 --> 00:06:00,968
Five hundred years ago, a
ship had no speedometer.

104
00:06:01,608 --> 00:06:03,288
Just the sea going past.

105
00:06:03,659 --> 00:06:07,679
So sailors threw a piece of wood
overboard, tied to a long rope.

106
00:06:08,339 --> 00:06:11,939
The wood stayed put in the water
while the ship sailed away from it.

107
00:06:12,483 --> 00:06:15,903
Later, knots were tied along
the rope at regular intervals.

108
00:06:16,723 --> 00:06:18,423
A sailor counted how many knots slipped

109
00:06:18,653 --> 00:06:21,303
overboard while a small
sand glass emptied.

110
00:06:21,674 --> 00:06:23,634
That number was the ship's speed.

111
00:06:23,714 --> 00:06:27,194
And that's why we still measure
the speed of ships in knots.

112
00:06:28,316 --> 00:06:30,316
The piece of wood was called the log.

113
00:06:30,576 --> 00:06:34,436
Every reading was written down,
first on a board, then in a book.

114
00:06:34,696 --> 00:06:36,196
The log book.

115
00:06:36,821 --> 00:06:40,421
Over time, the log book grew
into a full diary of the voyage.

116
00:06:41,081 --> 00:06:44,601
Speed, weather, position,
anything that happened on board.

117
00:06:44,797 --> 00:06:47,077
And when computers needed
a place to write down

118
00:06:47,307 --> 00:06:50,657
everything that happened,
the name was already waiting.

119
00:06:50,971 --> 00:06:52,771
Every line in a server log is a distant

120
00:06:53,001 --> 00:06:57,931
descendant of a sailor counting
knots on a rope. Just with less wood.

121
00:07:00,361 --> 00:07:02,241
Booting is even stranger.

122
00:07:02,717 --> 00:07:04,777
In the nineteenth
century, there was a joke

123
00:07:05,007 --> 00:07:07,997
about pulling yourself up
by your own bootstraps.

124
00:07:08,317 --> 00:07:10,657
The little loops at the top of a boot.

125
00:07:11,349 --> 00:07:13,329
It was meant to sound absurd.

126
00:07:14,049 --> 00:07:17,329
You can't lift yourself off the
ground by pulling on your own boots.

127
00:07:18,009 --> 00:07:20,609
It was a way of describing
something impossible.

128
00:07:21,390 --> 00:07:24,210
But a computer has to do
something very similar.

129
00:07:24,970 --> 00:07:28,530
When you switch it on, it needs
a program to load its programs.

130
00:07:29,410 --> 00:07:32,030
And loading programs is a program's job.

131
00:07:32,205 --> 00:07:35,385
So it starts with a tiny piece of
code that loads a slightly bigger

132
00:07:35,615 --> 00:07:40,225
one, which loads a bigger one still,
until the whole system is running.

133
00:07:40,898 --> 00:07:44,158
The computer pulls itself
up by its own bootstraps.

134
00:07:44,438 --> 00:07:48,038
Engineers called it bootstrapping,
and then just booting.

135
00:07:48,569 --> 00:07:50,689
A joke about the
impossible became the first

136
00:07:50,919 --> 00:07:53,629
thing every computer does
when you switch it on.

137
00:07:55,989 --> 00:07:57,329
And then there's the bug.

138
00:07:58,068 --> 00:08:00,048
You've probably heard the story.

139
00:08:00,588 --> 00:08:03,768
In 1947, the team working
on the Harvard Mark

140
00:08:03,998 --> 00:08:06,748
II computer found a moth stuck in a relay.

141
00:08:07,425 --> 00:08:09,485
They taped it into the
computer's notes, with a

142
00:08:09,715 --> 00:08:12,925
comment: "First actual
case of bug being found."

143
00:08:13,686 --> 00:08:15,586
Into the log book, of course.

144
00:08:16,385 --> 00:08:18,125
But look at that comment again.

145
00:08:18,805 --> 00:08:21,105
First actual case. It's a joke.

146
00:08:21,185 --> 00:08:24,905
And it only works if everyone
already knew what a bug was.

147
00:08:25,109 --> 00:08:26,309
And they did.

148
00:08:26,809 --> 00:08:32,129
In 1878, Thomas Edison was writing
about the bugs in his inventions, the

149
00:08:32,359 --> 00:08:35,989
little faults and difficulties that
show up when you build something new.

150
00:08:36,156 --> 00:08:38,256
So the moth didn't give us the word.

151
00:08:38,836 --> 00:08:41,536
It was just the first bug
you could actually hold.

152
00:08:43,896 --> 00:08:47,276
Now here's a word that sounds much
more sinister than it is. Daemon.

153
00:08:48,599 --> 00:08:51,599
On many computers, little
programs run quietly in the

154
00:08:51,829 --> 00:08:54,939
background, handling the
mail, the printer, the clock.

155
00:08:55,719 --> 00:08:57,179
Programmers call them daemons.

156
00:08:58,254 --> 00:08:59,974
Not because they're evil.

157
00:09:00,654 --> 00:09:05,214
The name came from MIT in the 1960s,
and it was borrowed from physics.

158
00:09:06,011 --> 00:09:11,931
In 1867, the physicist James Clerk Maxwell
imagined a tiny being, later called

159
00:09:12,161 --> 00:09:17,271
Maxwell's demon, guarding a little door
in a wall that split a box of gas in two.

160
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:20,840
It let the fast molecules
through one way, and the slow

161
00:09:21,070 --> 00:09:24,060
ones the other, quietly
sorting them, never resting.

162
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:27,680
A tireless little helper,
working in the background.

163
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:32,820
That's exactly what the programmers
at MIT wanted their programs to be.

164
00:09:33,014 --> 00:09:36,114
And in ancient Greek, a
daimon wasn't a devil at all.

165
00:09:36,634 --> 00:09:38,854
It was a spirit that watched over you.

166
00:09:41,024 --> 00:09:42,404
And finally, the worm.

167
00:09:43,103 --> 00:09:45,403
A computer worm is a
program that copies itself

168
00:09:45,633 --> 00:09:48,923
from one computer to the
next, all on its own.

169
00:09:49,492 --> 00:09:51,212
The name came from a novel.

170
00:09:51,972 --> 00:09:55,172
The Shockwave Rider, from 1975, in which a

171
00:09:55,402 --> 00:09:58,312
programmer turns a tapeworm
loose in a giant network.

172
00:09:59,041 --> 00:10:01,981
A few years later, researchers
at Xerox wrote real

173
00:10:02,211 --> 00:10:05,321
programs that spread between
the machines on their network.

174
00:10:05,681 --> 00:10:08,121
And they borrowed the name from the book.

175
00:10:08,760 --> 00:10:11,940
The same lab, as it happens,
that gave us the desktop.

176
00:10:14,670 --> 00:10:16,350
A log that was a piece of wood.

177
00:10:16,430 --> 00:10:19,190
Booting, which began as a joke.

178
00:10:19,910 --> 00:10:21,970
A bug that was older than computers.

179
00:10:22,730 --> 00:10:25,130
A daemon that was a physicist's helper.

180
00:10:25,510 --> 00:10:27,850
A worm from a science fiction novel.

181
00:10:28,849 --> 00:10:31,849
And that's really the answer
to the question in the title.

182
00:10:32,571 --> 00:10:35,311
Almost everything a
computer does is invisible.

183
00:10:36,031 --> 00:10:38,831
You can't see a packet,
or a buffer, or a cloud.

184
00:10:39,203 --> 00:10:45,163
So we name the invisible world after the
visible one. A desk. A river. A truck.

185
00:10:45,243 --> 00:10:47,503
A parcel. A rope with knots in it.

186
00:10:48,449 --> 00:10:51,489
And after a while, we forget
they were ever metaphors.

187
00:10:52,551 --> 00:10:55,131
We didn't invent a language for computers.

188
00:10:55,831 --> 00:10:58,531
We borrowed one from the
world we already understood.

189
00:10:59,726 --> 00:11:03,546
This turned out to be so much fun
that it would be a shame to stop here.

190
00:11:04,146 --> 00:11:07,056
So in the next video, we'll look
at a few more of these words.
